---
// Copy source: qualireps-v2/qualireps-audit/Landing page textes.md, section
// "Page How it works", checked against the app code (qualireps-v2 @ 810f662:
// src/lib/logic/dose.ts, reserve.ts, recovery.ts, criterion.ts, checkSet.ts,
// triggers.ts, triggerMessages.ts, load.ts, pastSet.ts, retestKind.ts;
// data/chains.ts; routes/ExerciseScreen.svelte, LogPastSet.svelte). Where
// the code and the document differed, the page follows the code. On-screen words are the app's own.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_ORIGIN } from '../lib/site';

const title = 'How it works — QualiReps';
const description =
  'How strength grows — nervous system first, then muscle — and the exact rules QualiReps uses: set size, reps left and rest, check sets, max tests, breaks.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE_ORIGIN}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'How it works', item: `${SITE_ORIGIN}/how-it-works` },
    ],
  },
];

// Each rule: a heading and a few plain sentences. HTML is limited to <em>
// for the app's on-screen labels. A rule with `paras` is explanatory prose
// rather than a list of rules.
type Rule = { h: string; items?: string[]; paras?: string[] };
const rules: Rule[] = [
  {
    h: 'How you get stronger',
    paras: [
      'Strength grows first in your nervous system. To move, your brain recruits motor units — groups of muscle fibers that fire together. Training teaches it to recruit them faster and better: more fibers at once, firing at a higher rate, working together. That speed and quality of recruitment is where most of your early progress comes from, and it is why strength belongs to the movement you practice. It builds through clean, repeated sets.',
      'Then the muscle follows. Asked again and again to work close to its limit, its fibers grow thicker — their number barely changes. This is hypertrophy, and it serves strength: bigger fibers give your nervous system more force to recruit. It comes more slowly, over months.',
      'QualiReps trains both from the same exercise. The low end of each range keeps sets short and crisp, far from failure; the high end brings each set closer to your max, where muscle grows.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Your max',
    items: [
      'Every exercise starts from a max: your best clean reps, or your best clean seconds for a hold.',
      'You set your max in one of four ways. A check set, the recommended one: as many clean reps as you can while keeping 2 in reserve; your max becomes those reps plus 2, higher or lower than before, with no 24-hour rest. A full test, to your limit, followed by 24 hours of recovery. A test you have already done. Or an estimate, marked as such, which the app suggests confirming with a check set. When you start an exercise, pick a level or load where you can do 5 to 10 clean reps: that is where sets size best. Holds, and maxes under 3, use a full test: a check set needs room for at least one rep with 2 left.',
      'Your max also rises when a set shows you can do more: the reps you did plus the reps you had left, marked estimated. Between full tests, your max can rise by up to 2 reps above your last full test, or above the max you started the level or load with; beyond that, the app asks for a full test. Only a check set or a test can lower it.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'The size of each set',
    items: [
      'Each next set is given as a range. Low end: half your max, rounded down. High end: your max minus 3, never below the low end. Every set leaves at least 2 reps in reserve.',
      'With a max of 1 or 2, no full set is proposed: the app suggests an easier variation (or a lighter load), slow negatives, or holding the hardest position. Single reps, well spaced, remain possible.',
      'Holds: half your max time, 30 seconds at most.',
      'One-sided exercises are sized from your weaker side.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Rest',
    items: [
      'After each set, tap how many reps you had left: 0, 1, 2 or 3+. The exercise then rests 2 minutes with 4 or more left, 3 minutes with 2 or 3, 5 minutes with 1. With 3+, the app uses how far the set was below your max.',
      '0 means a maximal effort: the exercise rests for 24 hours, and at or above your max it counts as a max test. If you tap 0 well below your max, the app asks whether it really was your limit.',
      'A hold that reaches your max: the app asks whether it was a true max effort.',
      'Rest is counted for each exercise on its own. The reserve buttons (or <em>Log set</em> for a hold) stay off until the exercise is <em>Fresh</em> again.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Checks and tests',
    items: [
      'Before a full test, warm up: a few minutes of light movement, then 2 or 3 short sets building up, a short rest, then test.',
      'A full test puts that exercise in <em>Recovery</em> for 24 hours. A check set does not. Your other exercises are not affected.',
      'A new full test at the same level waits 48 hours.',
      'A check set or an estimated max starts no recovery.',
      'About every three weeks, or after a solid volume of work, the app suggests a check set to keep your max current, never sooner than a week after the last one (a full test for holds and small maxes).',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'After a break',
    items: [
      'After 2 weeks without a set on an exercise, its sets are sized from 90% of your max, and from 80% after 4 weeks, until you record a new max.',
      'After 4 weeks, a check set is recommended (a full test for holds and small maxes). Nothing is blocked.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Levels and loads',
    items: [
      'Each level of a bodyweight path has a passage criterion. When your max reaches it, the app says so and offers <em>Change level</em>.',
      'Your own exercises with a load have a criterion you set: 10 reps by default. The kettlebell press uses 12, because bells go up in large steps. When your max reaches the criterion, the app suggests a heavier load.',
      'Changing level or load means setting a new max, with a check set if you like. If a heavier load would bring your max below 5, the app says so and suggests a smaller step, except for kettlebells, which go up in large steps.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Sets logged later',
    items: [
      '<em>Log a past set</em> records a set you forgot. It counts in your volume and is never refused: not during rest, not during recovery, not on a rest day.',
      'A past set cannot be dated in the future, or before your last check set or max test.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Rest days',
    items: [
      'Under Settings → <em>Active days</em>, you choose the days you train. Sunday is off by default.',
      'On a rest day, no set can be logged live. Past sets can still be added.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'What the app does not do',
    items: [
      'No program. No imposed goal. No automatic move to the next level or the next load.',
      'It never asks you to go to failure.',
      'The app shows when the next set is safe. What you train stays your decision.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/how-it-works" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="how">
      <header class="how-head">
        <div class="eyebrow">How it works</div>
        <h1>The rules, written down.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          QualiReps follows the same rules every day, for every exercise. Here
          they are, in plain words.
        </p>
      </header>

      <ol class="rules" role="list">
        {
          rules.map((rule, i) => (
            <li class="rule">
              <div class="rule-head">
                <span class="n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <h2>{rule.h}</h2>
              </div>
              {rule.paras ? (
                <div class="rule-prose">
                  {rule.paras.map((p) => (
                    <p set:html={p} />
                  ))}
                </div>
              ) : (
                <ul class="rule-body">
                  {rule.items!.map((item) => (
                    <li set:html={item} />
                  ))}
                </ul>
              )}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>

      <aside class="how-cta">
        <a href="/#install" class="btn btn-primary">Install QualiReps — free</a>
        <a href="/pricing" class="btn btn-ghost">Pricing</a>
      </aside>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .how {
    padding: clamp(32px, 5vw, 64px) 0 clamp(48px, 7vw, 96px);
  }
  .how-head {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin-bottom: clamp(32px, 5vw, 56px);
  }
  .how-head .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  .how h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .how .lead {
    font-size: clamp(16px, 1.5vw, 19px);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .rules {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline-soft);
  }
  .rule {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.6fr);
    gap: clamp(16px, 5vw, 72px);
    padding: clamp(24px, 3vw, 36px) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline-soft);
  }
  .rule-head {
    display: flex;
    gap: 14px;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .rule-head .n {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 600;
    min-width: 22px;
  }
  .rule-head h2 {
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.2vw, 26px);
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .rule-body {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .rule-body li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.8rem;
    color: var(--fg);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .rule-body li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .rule-body li::before {
    content: '—';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .rule-body li :global(em) {
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--accent-hi);
  }
  .rule-body li :global(strong) {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .rule-prose p {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    color: var(--fg);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    max-width: 68ch;
  }
  .rule-prose p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .how-cta {
    margin-top: clamp(32px, 5vw, 56px);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .rule {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
